WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.937
Discovery.

2
00:00:00.937 --> 00:00:05.002
The Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 07 Chapter 02

3
00:00:05.002 --> 00:00:09.238
points out that a CA database
shows only what that CA issued.

4
00:00:09.238 --> 00:00:14.116
Certificates from public CAs, appliances
or self-signing don't appear there,

5
00:00:14.116 --> 00:00:16.826
and finding them takes network discovery.

6
00:00:16.826 --> 00:00:18.775
The platform has five ways in.

7
00:00:18.775 --> 00:00:24.313
Network Discovery scans IPv4 ranges
and ports for TLS listeners,

8
00:00:24.313 --> 00:00:28.825
and can instantly check a
single IPv4 or IPv6 address.

9
00:00:28.825 --> 00:00:33.144
CA Import pulls what a Microsoft
CA or Amazon Private CA issued,

10
00:00:33.144 --> 00:00:38.625
template by template, optionally including
expired and revoked certificates.

11
00:00:38.625 --> 00:00:43.800
Onboard Discovery is agentless and brings
the application configuration with it.

12
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.020
And the Discovery Import API takes
results from a collector you write,

13
00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:49.912
such as the Graph job from Part 2.

14
00:00:49.912 --> 00:00:52.638
Reconciliation decides
whether a discovered

15
00:00:52.638 --> 00:00:55.363
certificate is one the
platform already knows.

16
00:00:55.363 --> 00:01:00.337
It matches on subject, key usage, extended
key usage and the DNS names in the

17
00:01:00.337 --> 00:01:05.475
subject alternative name, and the newer
certificate becomes the active one.

18
00:01:05.475 --> 00:01:08.875
It doesn't apply to CA
Import or Onboard Discovery.

19
00:01:08.875 --> 00:01:13.707
The program's rule, from Book 07
Chapter 08: every certificate found

20
00:01:13.707 --> 00:01:18.187
on a listener with no issuance
record and no owner is a finding.

21
00:01:18.187 --> 00:01:22.417
As design guidance for Wave 1,
run read-only network scans of

22
00:01:22.417 --> 00:01:27.250
server and appliance ranges, and
import the in-scope AD CS templates.

23
00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:30.417
That import needs the
privileged CA account,

24
00:01:30.417 --> 00:01:33.412
so create it under the
ESC7 controls first.

25
00:01:33.412 --> 00:01:37.899
Sources: CyberArk 26.1
Certificate discovery overview;

26
00:01:37.899 --> 00:01:44.532
Configuring Network Discovery; Certificate
reconciliation; Onboard Discovery;

27
00:01:44.532 --> 00:01:51.067
Agent discovery; Importing certificates
from a CA; WebSDK POST Discovery/Import;

28
00:01:51.067 --> 00:01:55.800
Book 07, Chapter 02 and Chapter
08; research brief section 9.
